  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Monday)\nIssued at 1229 PM MST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nCurrent upper air models and Water Vapor show mild ridge over the\nnorthern plains this afternoon, with a wave positioned over the\nRockies. 18Z surface analysis places the surface high\nover northeastern SD, and a surface low associated with the wave\nover northern CO this afternoon. Satellite shows mostly cloudy\nskies across the forecast area. Winds are light to breezy out of\nthe southeast, with temperatures in the upper 20s and 30s.\n\nLight winds will promote patchy areas of fog developing in the\nBlack Hills region tonight, however widespread fog is not\nexpected. Low level winds shift to out of the southwest and west\nfor Tuesday, promoting some warm air advection, with highs\ntrending into the 40s for Tuesday afternoon. Upper ridge builds \nover the Rockies mid- week, and we will see temperatures gradually\nwarming Tues- Thurs, with highs on Thursday and Friday back into \nthe 50s for most of the region. Late Tuesday night into Wednesday,\nmodels show an upper wave riding the ridge southeast into the\nDakotas. Limited moisture and timing will bring nothing more than\na wind shift as the disturbance moves through early Wednesday. \n\nLooking towards the weekend, upper flow becomes more\nnorthwesterly, with multiple weak disturbances riding the flow\nthrough the northern plains. This could result in a couple light\nprecipitation chances, but no meaningful accumulations are\nexpected.",
